    # NetworkManager profile for the operator's wireless network (SPEC.md
    # §spec:network-attachment). Appended to butane/kantainer.bu.tmpl's
    # storage.files list by render-ignition.sh, and only when the config names
    # a network - a wired machine carries no wireless configuration at all.
    #
    # 0600 root:root is required, not tidiness: NetworkManager ignores a keyfile
    # that any other account can read, and says nothing when it does.
    #
    # The filename is fixed rather than derived from the network name. An SSID
    # may contain slashes and spaces, and sanitising one into a filename is a
    # problem worth not having - the network is identified by the profile's own
    # ssid field. It follows that a machine has one wireless network, which is
    # what §req:constraints scopes: one operator, one machine.
    #
    # POWER SAVE IS OFF IN THAT PROFILE, and it is not a tuning preference.
    # NetworkManager's default leaves it to the driver, which on iwlwifi means
    # on. A station that sleeps between beacons receives broadcast frames only
    # after a DTIM beacon, and ARP requests are broadcast - so the machine
    # answers nobody's ARP, every inbound connection fails before it is made,
    # and nothing on the machine looks wrong: it reaches its gateway, holds its
    # lease, and reports a healthy link. Observed on real hardware, where a
    # machine sat awake and unreachable for ninety minutes with the login screen
    # still showing the address nobody could connect to.
    #
    # It is the wireless case that cannot tolerate this. A machine reached over
    # wireless has that one path, and SPEC.md §spec:remote-access is what an
    # operator is left with when it breaks - so the radio dozing is the
    # difference between a machine that is administrable and one that needs a
    # keyboard. The cost is a few hundred milliwatts on a machine that is
    # already running Docker.
    #
    # butane/wireless.nmconnection.tmpl sets no route metric or priority.
    # §spec:network-attachment wants the wired connection used when one is
    # present, and preferring wired over wireless is already what
    # NetworkManager does.
    - path: /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/kantainer-wireless.nmconnection
      mode: 0600
      user:
        id: 0
      group:
        id: 0
      contents:
        local: kantainer-wireless.nmconnection
